n   f(x) = x + \\frac{2}{x}\n   \\]\n\n7. To find the minimum, take the derivative of \\(f(x)\\) and set it to zero:\n\n   \\[\n   f'(x) = 1 - \\frac{2}{x^2}\n   \\]\n\n   Setting the derivative to zero:\n\n   \\[\n   1 - \\frac{2}{x^2} = 0 \\implies \\frac{2}{x^2} = 1 \\implies x^2 = 2 \\implies x = \\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n\n8. We confirm this is a minimum by checking the second derivative:\n\n   \\[\n   f''(x) = \\frac{4}{x^3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(f''(x) > 0\\) for \\(x = \\sqrt{2}\\), this is indeed a minimum.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThe minimum value of \\(AD\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{2}}\\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given the ellipse \\(\\frac{x^{2}}{4}+\\frac{y^{2}}{3}=1\\) with the left and right foci \\(F_{1}\\) and \\(F_{2}\\) respectively, a line \\(l\\) passes through the right focus and intersects the ellipse at points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\). Find the maximum area of the inscribed circle of \\(\\triangle F_{1}PQ\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out this problem step by step. The problem is about finding the maximum area of the inscribed circle (incircle) of triangle F1PQ, where F1 is the left focus of the given ellipse, and points P and Q are where a line l passes through the right focus F2 and intersects the ellipse. \n\nFirst, I need to recall some properties of ellipses. The standard equation of an ellipse is x\u00b2/a\u00b2 + y\u00b2/b\u00b2 = 1, where a is the semi-major axis and b is the semi-minor axis. In this case, the given ellipse is x\u00b2/4 + y\u00b2/3 = 1, so a\u00b2 = 4 and b\u00b2 = 3. Therefore, a = 2 and b = \u221a3. \n\nNext, I need to find the coordinates of the foci F1 and F2. For an ellipse, the distance from the center to each focus is c, where c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2. Plugging in the values, c\u00b2 = 4 - 3 = 1, so c = 1. Since the ellipse is centered at the origin (0,0), the foci are located at (-c, 0) and (c, 0), which means F1 is (-1, 