to the properties of the ellipse, leading to a semi-perimeter \\(s = 4\\).\n   - The inradius \\(r\\) is given by \\(r = \\frac{A}{s}\\).\n\n4. **Maximizing the Area**:\n   - The function \\(A = \\frac{12 \\sin\\theta}{3\\cos^2\\theta + 4\\sin^2\\theta}\\) is maximized by analyzing it in terms of \\(\\sin\\theta\\) and \\(\\cos\\theta\\). The maximum occurs at \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), where the line is vertical, yielding the maximum area \\(A = 3\\).\n\n5. **Maximum Inradius and Area of Incircle**:\n   - The maximum inradius \\(r\\) is \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\), leading to the maximum area of the inscribed circle as \\(\\pi \\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)^2 = \\frac{9}{16}\\pi\\).\n\nThus, the maximum area of the inscribed circle of \\(\\triangle F_1PQ\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{9}{16}\\pi}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the ellipse equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{3} = 1\n\\]\nThe foci of the ellipse, denoted as \\(F_1\\) and \\(F_2\\), can be found using the formula for the foci of an ellipse \\(\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\\):\n\n1. Identify \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n    \\[\n    a^2 = 4 \\quad \\text{(semi-major axis squared)}\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    b^2 = 3 \\quad \\text{(semi-minor axis squared)}\n    \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(c\\) using \\(c^2 = a^2 - b^2\\):\n    \\[\n    c^2 = a^2 - b^2 = 4 - 3 = 1 \\implies c = 1\n    \\]\n    The foci are at:\n    \\[\n    F_1(-1, 0), \\quad F_2(1, 0)\n    \\]\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution to Find the Largest Area:\n\nConsider a line \\(l\\) passing through the right focus \\(F_2\\). The general form of this line:\n\\[\nl: x = my + 1\n\\]\n\n### Finding Intercepts with the Ellipse\nSubstitute \\(x = my + 1\\) into the ellipse equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{(my + 1)^2}{4} + \\frac{y^2}{3} = 1\n\\]\n\nThis leads to:\n\\[\n\\frac{m^2 y^2 + 2my + 1}{4} + \\frac{y^2}{3} = 1\n\\]\n\nClearing the fraction, multiply through by 12:\n\\[\n3(m^2 y^2 + 2my + 1) + 4y^2 = 12\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\\[\n3m^2 y^2 + 6my + 3 + 4y^2 = 12\n\\]\n\\[\n(3m^2 + 4) y^2 + 6my - 9 = 0\n\\]\n\nLet the points of intersection be \\(P(x_1, y_1)\\) and \\(Q(x_2, y_2)\\). By Vieta's formulas:\n\\[\ny_1 + y_2 = -\\frac{6m}{3m^2 + 4}\n\\]\n\\[\ny_1 y_2 = -\\frac{9}{3m^2 + 4}\n\\]\n\n### Calculating the Area of \\(\\triangle F_1 PQ\\):\n\nThe area of \\(\\triangle F_1 PQ\\) can be given by:\n\\[\nS_{\\triangle F_1 PQ} = \\frac{1}{2} |F_1 F_2| |y_1 - y_2|\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\( |y_1 - y_2| \\):\n\\[ \n|y_1 - y_2| = \\sqrt{(y_1 + y_2)^2 - 4y_1 y_2}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\sqrt{\\left(-\\frac{6m}{3m^2 + 4}\\right)^2 - 4\\left(-\\frac{9}{3m^2 + 4}\\right)}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\sqrt{\\frac{36m^2}{(3m^2 + 4)^2} + \\frac{36}{3m^2 + 4}}\n\\]\n\\[\n= \\sqrt{\\frac{36(m^2 + 1)}{(3m^2 + 4)^2}}\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\nS_{\\triangle F_1 PQ} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{36(m^2 + 1)}{(3m^2 + 4)^2}}\n\\]\n\\[\n= 12 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{m^2 + 1}{(3m^2 + 4)^2}}\n\\]\n\nFurther, simplify:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{m^2 + 1}{(3m^2 + 4)^2}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{m^2 + 1}}{3m^2 + 4}\n\\]\n\\[\nS_{\\triangle F_1 PQ} = 12 \\frac{\\sqrt{m^2 + 1}}{3m^2 + 4}\n\\]\n\n### Maximizing the Area:\nTo maximize \\(\\frac{m^2 + 1}{(3m^2 + 4)^2}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{m^2 + 1}{(3m^2 + 4)^2} = \\frac{1}{(3m^2+4)^2} \\leq \\frac{1}{16}\n\\]\n\nFinally, the inradius \\( r \\) of the incircle is given by:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{2S_{\\triangle F_1 PQ}}{8} \\leq \\frac{3}{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum area of the incircle:\n\\[\nA = \\pi r^2 = \\pi \\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)^2 = \\frac{9\\pi}{16}\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\(\\boxed{\\frac{9\\pi}{16}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Calculate the sum of the following series:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1}+\\frac{2}{2}+\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{4}{8}+\\frac{5}{16}+\\ldots+\\frac{n}{2^{n-1}}\n$$\n\nWhat does the sum approach as the number of terms increases without bound?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to calculate the sum of this series: 1/1 + 2/2 + 3/4 + 4/8 + 