rac{1}{2})^2} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}} = 2\n$$\n\nThus, the sum \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{k}{2^k} = 2\\). Multiplying this result by 2 gives the sum of the original series:\n\n$$\n2 \\times 2 = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series approaches \\(\\boxed{4}\\) as the number of terms increases without bound.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Consider the given series and write it out explicitly:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\frac{k}{2^{k-1}} = \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{2}{2} + \\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{4}{8} + \\frac{5}{16} + \\ldots + \\frac{n}{2^{n-1}}\n   \\]\n\n2. To simplify the analysis, rewrite the summation with a common denominator:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\frac{k}{2^{k-1}} = \\frac{1 \\cdot 2^{n-1} + 2 \\cdot 2^{n-2} + 3 \\cdot 2^{n-3} + \\ldots + (n-1) \\cdot 2 + n}{2^{n-1}}\n   \\]\n\n3. Focus on simplifying the numerator separately. Break down individual terms of the form \\( k \\cdot 2^{n-k} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   2 \\cdot 2^{n-2} &= 2^{n-2} + 2^{n-2}, \\\\\n   3 \\cdot 2^{n-3} &= 2^{n-3} + 2^{n-3} + 2^{n-3}, \\\\\n   &\\vdots \\\\\n   (n-1) \\cdot 2 &= 2 + 2 + \\ldots + 2 \\quad \\text{(n-1 terms)}, \\\\\n   n \\cdot 1 &= 1 + 1 + \\ldots + 1 \\quad \\text{(n terms)}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n4. Rearrange the expression to group similar powers of \\(2\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   & (2^{n-1} + 2^{n-2} + \\ldots + 1) + (2^{n-2} + 2^{n-3} + \\ldots + 1) + \\ldots \\\\\n   & + (2^{n-3} + \\ldots + 1) + \\ldots + (2 + 1) + 1\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n5. Sum each geometric series within the brackets:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} 2^k = \\frac{2^n - 1}{2 - 1} = 2^n - 1\n   \\]\n\n6. Simplify the overall series:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^{n-2} 2^k + \\sum_{k=0}^{n-3} 2^k + \\ldots + 1 = (2^n - 1) + (2^{n-1} - 1) + (2^{n-2} - 1) + \\ldots + 1\n   \\]\n\n7. Combine terms:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{m=0}^{n-1} (2^{n-m} - 1) = 2^n + 2^{n-1} + 2^{n-2} + \\ldots + 2 + 1 - (n+1)\n   \\]\n\n8. This further simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   2^{n+1} - 1 - (n+1)\n   \\]\n\n9. Divide by \\(2^{n-1}\\) to get back to the original fraction's form:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2^{n+1} - (n + 2)}{2^{n-1}} = 4 - \\frac{n + 2}{2^{n-1}}\n   \\]\n\n10. Analyze the second term as \\( n \\to \\infty \\):\n    \\[\n    \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(4 - \\frac{n+2}{2^{n-1}}\\right) = 4\n    \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series approaches:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In the isosceles triangle \\(ABC\\), \\(\\angle ACB = \\angle ABC = 40^\\circ\\). On the ray \\(AC\\), mark off the segment \\(AD\\) such that \\(AD = BC\\). What is the measure of \\(\\angle BDC\\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out this geometry problem. So, we have an isosceles triangle ABC where angles at C and B are both 40 degrees. That means the triangle has two equal angles, so the sides opposite those angles should be equal. Wait, in a triangle, the sides opposite equal angles are equal. So if angles at B and C are both 40 degrees, then sides opposite them, which would be AC and AB, right? Wait, angle at B is 40\u00b0, so the side opposite is AC. Angle at C is 40\u00b0, so the side opposite is AB. Therefore, AC = AB. So triangle ABC is isosceles with AB = AC. Wait, but the problem says it's an isosceles triangle ABC. Usually, the order matters, so ABC is isosceles with base BC or something? Hmm, maybe I should clarify.\n\nWait, angles at B and C are both 40\u00b0, so angle at A must be 100\u00b0, since the sum of angles in a triangle is 180\u00b0. So angle BAC is 100\u00b0, and angles at ABC and ACB are 40\u00b0 each. Therefore, sides AB and AC are equal because their opposite angles are equal (angles at C and B). Wait, no, hold on. The side opposite angle B is AC, and the side opposite angle C is AB. Since angles B and C are equal, sides AC and AB are equal. Therefore, AB = AC. So t