o $C$.\n   - Since $P$ lies on the median $C M_3$, we have $P$ being equidistant from both $A$ and $B$ along the $C M_3$. The line segment $APC$ and $BPC$ must have equal area because the segment from $C$ to each triangle\u2019s base ($AP$ or $BP$) represents equal heights and the bases $AP$ and $BP$ are proportionally equal.\n   - Hence, the areas of $\\triangle APC$ and $\\triangle BPC$ are equal.\n\n5. **Comparing Areas of Triangles $CPA_1$ and $CPB_1$:**\n   - Lines $A P$ and $B P$ intersect $A C$ and $B C$ at $A_1$ and $B_1$ respectively.\n   - Since $P$ lies on the median $C M_3$ and lines $A P$ and $B P$ merely extend from $P$ to intersect $AC$ and $BC$, $P$ being on $C M_3$ ensures that the areas formed by $CPA_1$ and $CPB_1$ have their vertices shared along equal segments created by $P$.\n   - Hence, by dividing along equal median proportional heights from a common centroidal symmetry relative to $C$, the areas $\\triangle CPA_1$ and $\\triangle CPB_1$ are equal.\n\n### Conclusion\nThus, the areas of the triangles $AA_1C$ and $BB_1C$ are equal.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The areas of triangles } AA_1C \\text{ and } BB_1C \\text{ are equal}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Draw a right triangle and connect the vertex of the right angle with the midpoint of the hypotenuse. Show that in this way the triangle is divided into two triangles of equal area.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to show that if you draw a right triangle and connect the vertex of the right angle to the midpoint of the hypotenuse, it divides the triangle into two smaller triangles with equal area. Hmm, let me try to visualize this first.\n\nLet me start by drawing a right triangle. Let's call the right angle vertex point C, and the other two vertices A and B. So, triangle ABC is a right-angled triangle at C. The hypotenuse is AB. Now, the midpoint of the hypotenuse would be the point exactly halfway between A and