t prime.\n\nHow about 2, 5, 8.\n\n2 +5=7, 2+8=10, 5+8=13. 10 is not prime.\n\nWait, seems like whenever we include an even number (2), the sum of two odds gives even, which is not prime (except 2). If we use two evens, sum is even. If we use 2 and another even, sum is even. So perhaps there is no way around.\n\nWait, let's try using 1. Since 1 is odd, adding it to another number. Let's say 1, 2, 4: tried that. 1, 2, 6: 1+2=3, 1+6=7, 2+6=8. 8 isn't prime. 1, 2, 8: 1+8=9, which is not prime.\n\nWhat if we take 1, 4, 6: as before. 1+4=5, 1+6=7, 4+6=10. Nope.\n\nWhat if all three numbers are even except one is 2. Let's see 2, 4, 6: 2+4=6, not prime. 2, 4, 8: same problem.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using 1, 2, and another number. Let me try 1, 2, 3: 1+2=3, 1+3=4, 2+3=5. 4 is not prime.\n\n1, 2, 5: 1+2=3, 1+5=6, 2+5=7. 6 isn't prime.\n\n1, 2, 7: 1+2=3, 1+7=8, 2+7=9. 8 and 9 are not prime.\n\nAlternatively, maybe three numbers where one is even (but not 2). Wait, even numbers greater than 2 are even, so adding them to any other even would give even sum greater than 2, which isn't prime. So 4, 6, 8: all sums are even. Not good.\n\nWait, so maybe there's no solution? But the problem is asking if such numbers exist. Maybe not. Let me check another approach.\n\nSuppose we have three numbers a < b < c. All distinct. So a is at least 1, b at least 2, c at least 3. Let's consider the smallest possible triplet.\n\nStart with a=1. Then, we need b and c such that 1 + b, 1 + c, and b + c are all prime.\n\nLet's pick a=1, b=2. Then 1+2=3 (prime), 1 + c needs to be prime, so c must be such that 1 + c is prime. So c could be 2, but it has to be distinct. So c must be at least 4. Let's try c=4. Then 1+4=5 (prime), 2+4=6 (not prime). So that's bad. Next c=6: 1+6=7 (prime), 2+6=8 (not prime). Hmm. c=8: 1+8=9 (not prime). c=10: 1+10=11 (prime), 2+10=12 (not prime). Not working.\n\nAlternatively, pick a=1, b=4. Then 1+4=5 (prime). Then c must satisfy 1 + c is prime and 4 + c is prime. Let's pick c=6: 1+6=7, 4+6=10 (not prime). c=10: 1+10=11, 4+10=14 (not prime). c=12: 1+12=13, 4+12=16 (not prime). Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, a=1, b=6. 1+6=7 (prime). Then c must satisfy 1 + c is prime and 6 + c is prime. Let's try c=8: 1+8=9 (not prime). c=10: 1+10=11, 6+10=16. No. c=12: 1+12=13, 6+12=18. No. Not working.\n\nMaybe a=1 isn't working. Let's try a=2. Then we need b and c such that 2 + b, 2 + c, and b + c are prime.\n\nLet's try a=2, b=3. Then 2+3=5 (prime). Then c must satisfy 2 + c is prime and 3 + c is prime. Let's pick c=5: 2+5=7 (prime), 3+5=8 (not prime). c=7: 2+7=9 (not prime). c=9: 2+9=11, 3+9=12 (not prime). c=11: 2+11=13, 3+11=14 (not prime). Hmm.\n\na=2, b=5. Then 2+5=7. c needs to satisfy 2 + c and 5 + c are prime. Let's try c=8: 2+8=10 (not prime). c=6: 2+6=8 (not prime). c=12: 2+12=14 (not prime). Not helpful.\n\na=2, b=7. Then 2+7=9 (not prime). So no.\n\nWait, maybe a=3. Then b and c must be higher. a=3, b=4. 3+4=7 (prime). Then c must satisfy 3 + c and 4 + c are prime. Let's pick c=10: 3+10=13, 4+10=14 (not prime). c=8: 3+8=11, 4+8=12 (not prime). c=14: 3+14=17, 4+14=18 (not prime). Not working.\n\na=3, b=5. 3+5=8 (not prime). Bad.\n\na=3, b=8. 3+8=11 (prime). Then c needs 3 + c and 8 + c prime. Let's try c=10: 3+10=13, 8+10=18. Not prime. c=14: 3+14=17, 8+14=22. Not prime. Hmm.\n\nThis approach isn't working. Let me think if there's another way.\n\nSuppose we consider that one of the numbers has to be even. Since 2 is the only even prime, but the numbers themselves don't have to be prime, just their sums. So if we include 2 as one of the numbers, then the other numbers have to be odd. Let's denote the numbers as 2, p, q, where p and q are odd. Then:\n\n2 + p must be prime,\n2 + q must be prime,\np + q must be prime.\n\nBut p and q are odd, so p + q is even. Therefore, p + q must equal 2, which is impossible because p and q are at least 1. Wait, but p and q are distinct. So if p + q = 2, they would have to be 1 and 1, but they can't be the same. Therefore, even if one number is 2, the sum of the other two would be even and greater than 2, hence composite. Therefore, having 2 in the triplet leads to the sum of the other two numbers being even and composite. So that can't work.\n\nAlternatively, if the even number is not 2, say 4. Then the other two numbers would be odd. Let's take 4, p, q (odd). Then:\n\n4 + p must be prime,\n4 + q must be prime,\np + q must be prime (which is even + even? Wait, p and q are odd, so p + q is even. Therefore, same problem. p + q must be 2, which is impossible. So same issue. So regardless of the even number, if we have one even and two odds, their sum is even and can't be prime (except 2, which is too small). Therefore, no solution in this case.\n\nAlternatively, could all three numbers be even? Then all pairwise sums are even. The sums would have to be 2, but the smallest sum would be 2 + 4 = 6, which is not prime. So that's impossible.\n\nAlternatively, all three numbers are odd. Then all pairwise sums are even, which again would have to be 2. But the sum of two odds is at least 1 + 3 = 4, which is not prime. So that's impossible.\n\nSo in all cases, there is a contradiction. Therefore, it seems like such three numbers do not exist. But wait, let me check some sources or think again.\n\nWait, I recall that in some problems, numbers like 1, 2, and 3 are considered, but their sums aren't all prime. Wait, for example, 1, 2, 3: sums are 3, 4, 5. 4 isn't prime. If someone suggests 1, 2, 4: sums are 3, 5, 6. 6 isn't prime. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me consider if the numbers can include 1. For example, 1, 2, and 4: as above. But 2 + 4 = 6. Not prime. So no. 1, 2, 6: 1+2=3, 1+6=7, 2+6=8. 8 isn't prime.\n\nAlternatively, 1, 4, 6: sums 5, 7, 10. 10 isn't prime. \n\nIs there a combination where all three sums are prime? Let's see. Suppose we have three numbers where two are even and one is odd. Wait, but two even numbers sum to even (non-prime except 2). So that's no good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using three primes? Let's try primes 3, 5, 7. Sums: 8, 10, 12. All even, not prime. No. Primes 2, 3, 5: sums 5, 7, 8. 8 isn't prime. Primes 2, 5, 7: sums 7, 9, 12. 9 and 12 aren't prime. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe non-prime numbers. Let's try 3, 4, 8. Sums: 7, 11, 12. 12 isn't prime. 3, 4, 10: sums 7, 13, 14. 14 isn't prime. 5, 6, 8: sums 11, 13, 14. 14 isn't prime.\n\nAlternatively, 5, 8, 12: sums 13, 17, 20. 20 isn't prime. \n\nThis is frustrating. Let me think if there's a mathematical way to prove that such numbers can't exist.\n\nSuppose we have three numbers a < b < c. All are positive integers, distinct. Then, the three sums a+b, a+c, b+c must all be primes.\n\nCase 1: All three numbers are odd. Then, a+b, a+c, b+c are all even. So they must equal 2. But a and b are at least 1 and 3 (since distinct), so a + b is at least 4, which can't be 2. Contradiction.\n\nCase 2: Two odds and one even. Let the even be a. Then a is even, and b, c are odd. Then, a + b and a + c are odd (primes), but b + c is even. Therefore, b + c must be 2, which is impossible since b and c are at least 1 and 3, sum is 4.\n\nCase 3: Two evens and one odd. Let a and b be even, c odd. Then a + b is even, must be 2. But a and b are distinct even numbers, so the smallest sum is 2 + 4 = 6. Contradiction.\n\nCase 4: All three even. Then all sums are even, must be 2. But as above, the smallest sum is 6. Contradiction.\n\nTherefore, in all possible cases, we have a contradiction. Therefore, such three numbers do not exist.\n\nBut wait, hold on. What if one of the numbers is 1? Let's re-examine the parity. If one number is 1 (odd), and the other two numbers are such that one is even and one is odd. Wait, but then we would have two odds and one even. Let's say numbers are 1 (odd), 2 (even), and 4 (even). Then, sums: 1+2=3 (prime), 1+4=5 (prime), 2+4=6 (not prime). Not good. If we have 1 (odd), even (2), odd (3). Then sums: 1+2=3 (prime), 1+3=4 (not prime), 2+3=5 (prime). Still one composite sum.\n\nAlternatively, numbers 1, 4, 6: sums 5, 7, 10. Again, 10 is composite.\n\nAlternatively, numbers 1, 6, 8: sums 7, 9, 14. Both 9 and 14 composite.\n\nAlternatively, numbers 1, 2, 6: sums 3, 7, 8. 8 is composite.\n\nHmm. It seems that no matter how we arrange the numbers, if there's an even number involved, the sum of two evens or two odds will result in a composite number. If we try to avoid even numbers entirely, then all numbers are odd, leading to even sums which are composite. Therefore, no triplet of distinct natural numbers can satisfy the condition that all pairwise sums are prime.\n\nTherefore, the answer must be that such numbers do not exist.", "deepseek_solution": "**Step-by-Step Explanation and Answer:**\n\nTo determine if three distinct natural numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) exist such that the sum of any two is prime, we analyze the parity (evenness/oddness) of the numbers and their sums:\n\n1. **Parity Constraints**:\n   - **Odd + Odd = Even**: The only even prime is 2. For two distinct odd numbers, their sum must be at least \\(1 + 3 = 4\\), which is not prime.\n   - **Even + Even = Even**: Similarly, the sum of two distinct even numbers is at least \\(2 + 4 = 6\\), which is not prime.\n   - **Odd + Even = Odd**: This sum could be prime, but requires careful pairing.\n\n2. **Case Analysis**:\n   - **All Odd Numbers**: If \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are all odd, all pairwise sums are even and greater than 2, making them composite. **Not possible**.\n   - **Two Odd, One Even**: Let \\(a\\) be even, \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) odd. Then \\(b + c\\) is even and must equal 2, but distinct odd numbers \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) sum to at least \\(1 + 3 = 4\\). **Not possible**.\n   - **Two Even, One Odd**: Let \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) be even, \\(c\\) odd. Then \\(a + b\\) is even and must equal 2, but distinct even numbers sum to at least \\(2 + 4 = 6\\). **Not possible**.\n   - **All Even Numbers**: All pairwise sums are even and greater than 2. **Not possible**.\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - Every combination of three distinct natural numbers leads to at least one non-prime sum due to parity constraints. Thus, **no such triplet exists**.\n\n**Final Answer**:  \nNo, there do not exist three distinct natural numbers such that the sum of any two is prime.", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem:\nExistence question: Are there three distinct natural (positive integer) numbers such that the sum of any two of them is a prime number?\n\n1. **Prime Number Observation**:\n    - Note that the number 2 is the only even prime number.\n    - All other prime numbers are odd.\n\n2. **Sum Constraints**:\n    - Consider three numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\).\n    - We need the sums \\(a + b\\), \\(a + c\\), and \\(b + c\\) to all be prime numbers.\n\n3. **Parity Analysis**:\n    - Since 2 is the only even prime number, at least one sum, say \\(a + b\\), must be odd because two odd numbers cannot add up to an even number, unless both are even.\n    - Therefore, \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) must be considered such that they involve both even and odd numbers to satisfy \\(a + b\\) as an odd number.\n\n4. **Establishing Number Types**:\n    - Let\u2019s assume \\(a\\) is even and both \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are odd (same reasoning applies if any substitution is made):\n      * \\(a + b\\) is odd prime.\n      * \\(a + c\\) is odd prime.\n    - Now consider the sum \\(b + c\\):\n      Since both \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are odd, \\(b + c\\) will be an even number, but for it to be a prime number, it must be 2 since 2 is the only even prime.\n\n5. **Contradiction**:\n    - Given that \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are positive odd integers, \\(b + c\\) should be greater than 2 (since each odd number is at least 1).\n    - Hence, it\u2019s impossible for \\(b + c\\) to equal 2.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThus, there are no three distinct natural numbers such that the sum of any two of them is a prime number.\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\text{No such numbers exist.}} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let $p$ be a prime number and $q$ be a prime divisor of $p^{p-1}+\\ldots+ p+1$. Show that $q \\equiv 1 (\\bmod p)$.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to show that if q is a prime divisor of this number p^{p-1} + p^{p-2} + ... + p + 1, then q is congruent to 1 modulo p. Hmm, let me start by understanding the problem better.\n\nFirst, p is a prime, and q is a prime that divides this sum. The sum itself looks like a geometric series. Wait, right, the sum of p^k from k=0 to p-1. Because p^{p-1} + ... + p + 1 is the same as (p^p - 1)/(p - 1) if I remember the formula for geometric series correctly. Let me check that. The sum of a geometric series from k=0 to n-1 is (r^n - 1)/(r - 1). So here, the common ratio is p, and the number of terms is p. So yes, the sum S = (p^p - 1)/(p - 1). So S is equal to that. Therefore, any prime divisor q of S must satisfy q \u2261 1 mod p. That's what I need to prove.\n\nAlright, so maybe I can use some properties from number theory here. Since q divides S, then (p^p - 1)/(p - 1) \u2261 0 mod q. Which means p^p \u2261 1 mod q, right? Because (p^p - 1) \u2261 0 mod q*(p - 1), but actually, q divides S = (p^p - 1)/(p - 1). So q divides (p^p - 1) but does not divide p - 1? Wait, not necessarily. Wait, if q divides both (p^p -1) and p -1, then q could divide their gcd. But maybe we can show that q cannot divide p -1, hence forcing p^p \u2261 1 mod q, and then use Fermat's little theorem or something.\n\nWait, let's go step by step.\n\nFirst, since q divides S = (p^p - 1)/(p - 1), then (p^p - 1) \u2261 0 mod q*(p - 1). But actually, q divides (p^p - 1)/(p - 1). So (p^p - 1) \u2261 0 mod q. But (p^p - 1) = (p - 1)*S, so since q divides S, then q divides (p^p -1). So p^p \u2261 1 mod q. So the order of p modulo q divides p and also divides q-1 by Fermat's little theorem. Wait, Fermat's little theorem says p^{q-1} \u2261 1 mod q, assuming q \u2260 p. Since q is a prime divisor of S, and S is (p^p -1)/(p -1), unless q divides p -1, but perhaps we can show that q cannot divide p -1, hence q \u2260 p, so Fermat applies.\n\nWait, suppose q divides p -1. Then q divides p -1, but also q divides S. Let me see if that's possible. If q divides p -1, then p \u2261 1 mod q. Then S = 1 + p + p^2 + ... + p^{p-1}. If p \u2261 1 mod q, then each term p^k \u2261 1 mod q, so S \u2261 p mod q. Since there are p terms each congruent to 1, so S \u2261 p mod q. Therefore, S \u2261 0 mod q implies p \u2261 0 mod q. But if q divides p -1, then q \u2264 p -1, so q cannot divide p unless q = p. But p is prime, so the only divisors are 1 and p. But p divides S? Let me check. If q = p, then S = (p^p -1)/(p -1). Let's see if p divides that. Let me compute S modulo p. Each term p^k \u2261 0 mod p for k \u22651, so S = 1 + 0 + 0 + ... + 0 = 1 mod p. So S \u2261 1 mod p, so p does not divide S. Therefore, q cannot be p, so if q divides S, then q \u2260 p, hence q divides (p^p -1) but not p -1. Wait, but if q divides both p^p -1 and p -1, then q divides gcd(p^p -1, p -1). Let's compute that gcd.\n\nCompute gcd(p^p -1, p -1). Let me write p^p -1 as (p^{p} -1). Let's use the formula gcd(a^n -1, a^m -1) = a^{gcd(n,m)} -1. So here, a = p, n = p, m =1. Then gcd(p^p -1, p -1) = p^{gcd(p,1)} -1 = p^1 -1 = p -1. Therefore, gcd(p^p -1, p -1) = p -1. Therefore, if q divides both p^p -1 and p -1, then q divides p -1. But S = (p^p -1)/(p -1), so if q divides S and q divides p -1, then q divides the numerator and the denominator. But S is (p^p -1)/(p -1). Therefore, if q divides both p^p -1 and p -1, then q^2 divides p^p -1. But since q divides p -1, let's see if that's possible. Let me take an example. Let p = 3. Then S = (3^3 -1)/(3 -1) = (27 -1)/2 = 26/2 =13. So q =13, which is \u22611 mod 3, which is 13 \u22611 mod 3, since 13-1=12, which is divisible by 3. So that works. Another example, p=5. S=(5^5 -1)/4=(3125 -1)/4=3124/4=781. Let's factor 781. 781 \u00f711=71, because 11*71=781. So q=11 and q=71. Check 11 mod 5: 11-1=10, which is divisible by 5, so 11\u22611 mod5. Similarly 71-1=70, divisible by5, so 71\u22611 mod5. So that holds. But if q divides p -1, then in the case of p=3, p -1=2. So S=13, which is not divisible by 2, so that's okay. For p=5, p -1=4. S=781. 781 is odd, so 2 does not divide it, and 4 does not divide 781. So in these examples, q does not divide p -1.\n\nTherefore, perhaps in general, if q divides S=(p^p -1)/(p -1), then q does not divide p -1. Let's suppose that q divides both S and p -1. Then, as before, S = (p^p -1)/(p -1). If q divides p -1, then p \u22611 mod q. Then S \u2261 1 +1 + ... +1 (p times) \u2261 p mod q. Therefore, S \u22610 mod q implies p \u22610 mod q. But q divides p -1, so q \u2264 p -1. Therefore, q divides p -1 and q divides p. But since p is prime, q must be 1 or p. But q is prime, so q=p. But earlier, we saw that S \u22611 mod p, so p does not divide S. Therefore, this is a contradiction. Therefore, q cannot divide p -1. Therefore, q does not divide p -1. Therefore, the gcd(q, p -1)=1. Hence, since q divides (p^p -1), and gcd(q, p -1)=1, then...\n\nWait, so from p^p \u22611 mod q and Fermat's little theorem, since q does not divide p (because q divides S and we saw that S \u22611 mod p, so q cannot be p), so p and q are coprime. Therefore, by Fermat's little theorem, p^{q-1} \u22611 mod q. So the multiplicative order of p modulo q divides both p and q-1. Since p is prime, the order is either 1 or p. If the order is 1, then p \u22611 mod q. But if p \u22611 mod q, then as before, S \u2261p mod q. Since S \u22610 mod q, that would imply p \u22610 mod q, which would mean q divides p. But p is prime, so q=p, which is impossible because S \u22611 mod p. Therefore, the order cannot be 1, so it must be p. Therefore, p divides q -1. Hence, q \u22611 mod p. Therefore, that's the conclusion.\n\nWait, let me verify each step again. So given that q divides S=(p^p -1)/(p -1), then p^p \u22611 mod q. Also, since q is prime, and p is a prime not equal to q (since q divides S and S \u22611 mod p, so q \u2260 p), then by Fermat\u2019s little theorem, p^{q-1} \u22611 mod q. Therefore, the multiplicative order of p modulo q divides both p and q-1. Since p is prime, the order is either 1 or p. If the order is 1, then p \u22611 mod q. But if p \u22611 mod q, then S =1 + p + p^2 + ... + p^{p-1} \u22611 +1 + ... +1 = p mod q. Therefore, S \u22610 mod q implies p \u22610 mod q. But q divides p, so q=p, which is a contradiction because S \u22611 mod p. Therefore, the order cannot be 1, hence the order is p. Therefore, p divides q -1, which implies q \u22611 mod p. Therefore, done.\n\nYes, that seems to hold. Let me check with the examples again. For p=3, q=13, which is 13\u22611 mod3, since 13-1=12 divisible by3. For p=5, q=11 and 71, both 11\u22611 mod5 (11-1=10) and 71-1=70, which is divisible by5. So that works. Another example, p=2. Then S=(2^2 -1)/(2 -1)=3/1=3. Then q=3, which is \u22611 mod2, since 3\u22611 mod2. So that works as well. So in all cases, the prime divisors are \u22611 mod p. Therefore, the reasoning seems solid.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that any prime divisor q of S=(p^p -1)/(p -1) must satisfy q\u22611 mod p. The key steps were recognizing the geometric series, using Fermat's little theorem, considering the multiplicative order of p modulo q, and showing that the order must be p, leading to the conclusion that p divides q-1.", "deepseek_solution": "To demonstrate that a prime divisor \\( q \\) of \\( S = p^{p-1} + p^{p-2} + \\cdots + p + 1 \\) satisfies \\( q \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p} \\), follow these steps:\n\n1. **Express \\( S \\) as a Geometric Series**:\n   \\[\n   S = \\sum_{k=0}^{p-1} p^k = \\frac{p^p - 1}{p - 1}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\( q \\) divides \\( S \\), it follows that \\( q \\) divides \\( p^p - 1 \\), i.e., \\( p^p \\equiv 1 \\pmod{q} \\).\n\n2. **Apply Fermat's Little Theorem**:\n   If \\( q \\neq p \\), by Fermat's theorem, \\( p^{q-1} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{q} \\). The order of \\( p \\) modulo \\( q \\) divides both \\( p \\) (from \\( p^p \\equiv 1 \\pmod{q} \\)) and \\( q-1 \\). Since \\( p \\) is prime, the order is either \\( 1 \\) or \\( p \\).\n\n3. **Exclude Order 1**:\n   If the order were \\( 1 \\), then \\( p \\equiv 1 \\pmod{q} \\). Substituting into \\( S \\):\n   \\[\n   S \\equiv 1 + 1 + \\cdots + 1 = p \\pmod{q}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\( S \\equiv 0 \\pmod{q} \\), this implies \\( p \\equiv 0 \\pmod{q} \\), contradicting \\( q \\neq p \\). Thus, the order must be \\( p \\).\n\n4. **Conclude \\( q \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p} \\)**:\n   The order \\( p \\) divides \\( q - 1 \\), so \\( q \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p} \\).\n\n**Final Answer**: Any prime divisor \\( q \\) of \\( S \\) satisfies \\( q \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p} \\). \\(\\boxed{q \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **D\u00e9finition et formulation initiale**:\n   \n   Soit \\( p \\) un nombre premier et \\( q \\) un diviseur premier de \\( S = p^{p-1} + \\ldots + p + 1 \\). Apr\u00e8s r\u00e9\u00e9criture, \\( S \\) peut \u00eatre vu comme une somme g\u00e9om\u00e9trique :\n   \\[\n   S = \\frac{p^p - 1}{p - 1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lien entre \\( q \\) et \\( p \\)**:\n   \n   Puisque \\( q \\) divise \\( S \\), on obtient que \\( q \\) divise \u00e9galement \\( p^p - 1 \\). ainsi \\( q \\mid p^p - 1 \\).\n\n3. **D\u00e9finition de l'ordre de \\( p \\) modulo \\( q \\)**:\n   \n   L'ordre de \\( p \\) modulo \\( q \\), not\u00e9 \\( d \\), est le plus petit entier \\( d \\) tel que \\( p^d \\equiv 1 \\pmod{q} \\). Puisque \\( q \\mid p^p - 1 \\), alors \\( d \\) divise \\( p \\) :\n   \\[\n   d \\mid p\n   \\]\n\n4. **Cas pour la valeur de \\( d \\)**:\n   \n   Puisque \\( d \\) divise \\( p \\), \\( d \\) peut \u00eatre soit 1 soit \\( p \\) :\n\n   - Si \\( d = 1 \\) :\n     \\[ \n     p^1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{q} \n     \\]\n     Ce qui veut dire que \\( q \\) diviserait \\( p - 1 \\). Cependant, nous savons que \n     \\[\n     p^{p-1} + \\ldots + p + 1 \\equiv 1 + \\ldots + 1 \\equiv p \\pmod{p-1}\n     \\]\n     ce qui simplifie \u00e0 :\n     \\[\n     p-1 \\text{ et } p^{p-1} + \\ldots + p + 1 \\text{ sont premiers entre eux}\n     \\]\n     et donc il est impossible que \\( q \\) divise les deux, on a donc contradiction.\n   \n   - Si \\( d = p \\) :\n     \\[\n     p^p \\equiv 1 \\pmod{q}\n     \\]\n     Par le Petit Th\u00e9or\u00e8me de Fermat, on obtient que :\n     \\[\n     p^{q-1} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{q}\n     \\]\n     Ainsi, \\( p^{\\text{gcd}(q-1, p)} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{q} \\). Puisque \\( d = p \\), alors \\( p \\mid q-1 \\), c'est-\u00e0-dire :\n     \\[\n     q \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}\n     \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{q \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given natural numbers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\), and an odd integer \\( z \\), it is known that \\( x! + y! = 48z + 2017 \\). Find all possible triples of numbers \\( (x, y, z) \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out how to solve this problem. We need to find all triples of natural numbers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\), and an odd integer \\( z \\), such that \\( x! + y! = 48z + 2017 \\). Hmm, factorials and modular equations. Let's start by recalling some properties of factorials and modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, factorials grow really quickly. So \\( x! \\) and \\( y! \\) will get large even for relatively small values of \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). Since the right-hand side is \\( 48z + 2017 \\), which is linear in \\( z \\), but \\( z \\) can be any odd integer. However, \\( x! \\) and \\( y! \\) are natural numbers, so \\( z \\) must also be a natural number because the left side is positive.\n\nWait, the problem says \\( z \\) is an odd integer. But since \\( x! + y! - 2017 = 48z \\), the left side must be divisible by 48. Also, \\( z \\) must be a positive integer because \\( x! \\) and \\( y! \\) are a