tric Equation**:\n   The equation \\(\\cos x = \\sin x\\) holds true when \\(x = 45^\\circ\\) or \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) radians because:\n\n   \\[\n   \\cos 45^\\circ = \\sin 45^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, we have:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\alpha + \\beta}{2} = 45^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n6. **Find the Sum \\(\\alpha + \\beta\\)**:\n   Multiply both sides by 2:\n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha + \\beta = 90^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n   In any triangle, the sum of the internal angles is \\(180^\\circ\\). Since \\(\\alpha + \\beta = 90^\\circ\\), the third angle \\(\\gamma\\) must satisfy:\n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   90^\\circ + \\gamma = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\gamma = 90^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n7. **Conclusion**:\n   Since one of the angles in the triangle is \\(90^\\circ\\), the triangle must be a right triangle.\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Yes, the triangle is necessarily a right triangle}}\n   \\]\n\n", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Replace the two asterisks with two different numbers such that the resulting equation is an identity:\n\n$$\n(3x - *)(2x + 5) - x = 6x^2 + 2(5x - *)\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out which two numbers replace the asterisks in the equation (3x - *)(2x + 5) - x = 6x\u00b2 + 2(5x - *) to make it an identity. Hmm, identities hold true for all values of x, so the coefficients of the corresponding terms on both sides must be equal. Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I'll start by expanding the left side of the equation. The left side is (3x - a)(2x + 5) - x, where 'a' is the first asterisk. Let me expand that product. Using the distributive property (FOIL method):\n\n(3x)(2x) + (3x)(5) - a(2x) - a(5) - x\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n3x * 2x = 6x\u00b2\n3x * 5 = 15x\n-a * 2x = -2a x\n-a * 5 = -5a\nThen subtract x at the end.\n\nSo combining all these terms:\n\n6x\u00b2 + 15x - 2a x - 5a 