\)}\n\n\\[ \nm = 255 - 70 = 185 \n\\]\n\nThus, the number of sets \\( A \\) satisfying the conditions is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{186}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "What is the maximum possible number of rays in space originating from a single point and forming obtuse angles pairwise?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so the problem is asking for the maximum number of rays that can originate from a single point in space such that every pair of rays forms an obtuse angle. Hmm, let's start by understanding what an obtuse angle between two rays means. An obtuse angle is one that's greater than 90 degrees but less than 180 degrees. Since the rays are in space, we're dealing with three dimensions here. \n\nFirst, maybe I should recall some basics about angles between vectors. If we have two rays starting from the same point, the angle between them can be found using the dot product of their direction vectors. Specifically, if two vectors u and v have an angle \u03b8 between them, then the dot product u\u00b7v = |u||v|cos\u03b8. Since we're dealing with rays, we can assume the direction vectors are unit vectors for simplicity. So the dot product would just be cos\u03b8. For the angle to be obtuse, cos\u03b8 must be negative because cos\u03b8 < 0 when \u03b8 is between 90 and 180 degrees. Therefore, the condition is that the dot product of any two direction vectors is negative.\n\nSo the problem reduces to finding the maximum number of unit vectors in three-dimensional space such that the dot product of any two is negative. Now, I need to figure out how many such vectors can exist. Let me think about this in lower dimensions first, maybe in 2D, to get some intuition.\n\nIn 2D, suppose we have rays from a point forming obtuse angles pairwise. Let's try to place as many vectors as possible. In 2D, the maximum number is 3. If you try to place three vectors, each pair must form an obtuse angle. For example, you c