ations are exactly \\(\\frac{d}{2}\\), achieving equality.\n\n### Final Answers\n\n(a) \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{d}{2}}\\)\n\n(b) \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{d}{2}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n#### Part (a):\n\n1. Let $d = d_g$ for some $1 \\leq g \\leq n$. Recall the definitions of \\(a_p\\) and \\(a_r\\):\n   \\[\n   a_p = \\max \\{a_j: 1 \\leq j \\leq g\\},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a_r = \\min \\{a_j: g \\leq j \\leq n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. Clearly, \n   \\[\n   1 \\leq p \\leq g \\leq r \\leq n \\quad \\text{and} \\quad d = a_p - a_r.\n   \\]\n\n3. For any sequence of real numbers $x_1 \\leq x_2 \\leq \\cdots \\leq x_n$, note that\n   \\[\n   (a_p - x_p) + (x_r - a_r) = (a_p - a_r) + (x_r - x_p) \\geq a_p - a_r = d.\n   \\]\n\n4. Therefore, \n   \\[\n   a_p - x_p \\geq \\frac{d}{2} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x_r - a_r \\geq \\frac{d}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n5. We thus have:\n   \\[\n   \\max \\left\\{ |x_i - a_i| : 1 \\leq i \\leq n \\right\\} \\geq \\max \\left\\{ |x_p - a_p|, |x_r - a_r| \\right\\} \\geq \\frac{d}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n6. Conclusion:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\max \\left\\{ |x_i - a_i| : 1 \\leq i \\leq n \\right\\} \\geq \\frac{d}{2}}.\n   \\]\n\n#### Part (b):\n\n1. To prove that there exists a sequence \\(x_1 \\leq x_2 \\leq \\cdots \\leq x_n\\) such that \n   \\[\n   \\max \\left\\{ |x_i - a_i| : 1 \\leq i \\leq n \\right\\} = \\frac{d}{2},\n   \\]\n   we construct the sequence $\\{x_k\\}$ as follows:\n   \\[\n   x_1 = a_1 - \\frac{d}{2}, \\quad x_k = \\max \\{x_{k-1}, a_k - \\frac{d}{2}\\}, \\quad (2 \\leq k \\leq n).\n   \\]\n\n2. We need to show that for this sequence, the inequality in part (a) holds as an equality.\n\n3. From the definition of $\\{x_k\\}$, it is clear that $\\{x_k\\}$ is non-decreasing and\n   \\[\n   x_k - a_k \\geq -\\frac{d}{2} \\quad \\text{for all } 1 \\leq k \\leq n.\n   \\]\n\n4. For any $1 \\leq k \\leq n$, let $l \\leq k$ be the smallest subscript such that $x_k = x_l$. Then either $l=1$, or $l \\geq 2$ and $x_l > x_{l-1}$.\n\n5. In both cases, we have:\n   \\[\n   x_k = x_l = a_l - \\frac{d}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n6. Because\n   \\[\n   a_l - a_k \\leq \\max \\{a_j: 1 \\leq j \\leq k\\} - \\min \\{a_j: k \\leq j \\leq n\\} = d_k \\leq d,\n   \\]\n   we obtain:\n   \\[\n   x_k - a_k = a_l - a_k - \\frac{d}{2} \\leq d - \\frac{d}{2} = \\frac{d}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n7. Thus, we have:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{d}{2} \\leq x_k - a_k \\leq \\frac{d}{2} \\quad \\text{for all } 1 \\leq k \\leq n.\n   \\]\n\n8. Conclusion:\n   \\[\n   \\max \\left\\{ |x_i - a_i| : 1 \\leq i \\leq n \\right\\} \\leq \\frac{d}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n9. From part (a), we know that the above bound is indeed $\\frac{d}{2}$, thus:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\max \\left\\{ |x_i - a_i| : 1 \\leq i \\leq n \\right\\} = \\frac{d}{2}}.\n   \\]\n\nBoth parts (a) and (b) are thus confirmed. The solution is now complete.", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Inside a sphere with radius \\( R \\), \\( n \\) points are located. Prove that the sum of the squares of the pairwise distances between them does not exceed \\( n^2 R^2 \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that inside a sphere of radius R, if there are n points, then the sum of the squares of all the pairwise distances between them is at most n\u00b2R\u00b2. Hmm, let's think about how to approach this. \n\nFirst, I recall that when dealing with points in a sphere and distances between them, coordinates might be a good way to go. Maybe I can represent each point as a vector in 3D space (assuming the sphere is in 3D, but maybe the dimension doesn't matter here). Let me try to generalize. Suppose each point is a vector in some d-dimensional space, since spheres exist in any dimension. But maybe the problem is in 3D? The problem doesn't specify, so perhaps the result holds regardless of the dimension. That might complicate things, but maybe there's a way to handle it without getting into specific dimensions.\n\nSo, if I have n points inside a sphere of 